insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 1, 'What quantity does STOIIP report?', '["The volume of oil that can be recovered from the reservoir over field life, once a recovery factor has been applied","The volume of oil in place measured at reservoir conditions, before any shrinkage is applied","The volume of oil the reservoir held before any production, expressed at surface conditions","The total pore volume of the reservoir, counting the pore space that holds water as well as the part that holds oil"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Stock tank oil initially in place is the oil present before production, brought to surface conditions by dividing hydrocarbon pore volume by the formation volume factor. Recoverable volume is a later number that applies a recovery factor, and hydrocarbon pore volume is the reservoir condition figure that STOIIP is converted from.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 2, 'Why does an error in the mapped surface arrive in a gross rock volume magnified rather than averaged away?', '["Because volume is a sum over every cell, so a shallow bias in the map adds rock at every cell in that area","Because the thin-plate spline amplifies any depth error as it interpolates, so an error at one well is returned larger at the nodes that sit between the six wells","Because the property constants at this tier multiply the geometry by a factor greater than one","Because the extrapolation limit forces the error outward to the edges of the map"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gross rock volume is an integral over area, with each of the 169 oil-bearing cells contributing a block of rock to a running total, so the contributions of a systematic surface error add together instead of cancelling. Reading a single depth off the same map carries an error of the same size only once, and the spline itself does not inflate the error it is fitted to.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 3, 'The Ekene booking at the 1560 m contact gives a STOIIP of 12.139208 MMstb. The laboratory then revises porosity from 0.20 to 0.10 and nothing else changes. What is the new STOIIP?', '["Unchanged, because porosity affects pore volume but not oil volume","Half of 12.139208 MMstb","A quarter of 12.139208 MMstb, because porosity enters twice in the chain","It cannot be stated without regridding the surfaces"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At the Associate tier every step after gross rock volume is a plain multiplication by a constant, so STOIIP scales linearly with porosity and halving it halves the answer. Nothing about the geometry changes, so no regridding is involved.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 4, 'Which statement about Ekene-2 at the 1560 m contact is correct?', '["It has the thinnest sand in the field, 25 m, and therefore holds no oil at the contact","It has an oil column of 36 m, the tallest in the field","It has 36 m of sand and an oil column of 36 m, since the sand is entirely oil filled","It has the thickest sand in the field at 36 m and an oil column of zero"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-2 carries 36 m of sand, the thickest in the fixture, but its TOP_SAND pick at 1565 m lies below the contact, so the whole section is in the water leg. Reading the 36 m as an oil column is the classic confusion of the sand isochore with the oil column.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 5, 'TOP_SAND has 201 live nodes and BASE_SAND has 201 live nodes, and the number of nodes where both are live is also 201. Why do the two surfaces share one mask?', '["Because the mask is decided by the control geometry, and both surfaces were built from the same six well coordinates","Because the two surfaces were gridded on the same 25 by 20 frame of 500 nodes, so any node that is live on one grid is live on the other","Because BASE_SAND was generated as a constant thickness offset below TOP_SAND, so it inherits the TOP_SAND mask by construction","Because the engine copies the TOP_SAND mask onto BASE_SAND before the volumes are summed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A node is live if it passes the hull test and lies within 800 m of a control point, and every Ekene well picked both tops, so the distance test returns the same answer on both grids. A shared frame alone would not guarantee a shared mask, and BASE_SAND is gridded from its own six picks.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 6, 'The Ekene frame at a 100 m cell has 25 columns by 20 rows. What does the resulting figure of 500 describe?', '["The number of nodes that hold oil at the 1560 m contact","The number of nodes that survive the 800 m extrapolation limit","The number of cells needed to cover the oil area of 1.69 km2","The number of nodes in the frame before any masking is applied"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The frame holds 500 nodes, of which 201 survive the mask and become live, and 169 of those hold oil at the 1560 m contact. Each of the three numbers answers a different question and they are quoted separately for that reason.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 7, 'Four extreme values are reported from the two Ekene grids: TOP_SAND deepest 1590, BASE_SAND crest 1570, BASE_SAND deepest 1615, and TOP_SAND crest 1539.7181396484375. Which of them is not a well pick?', '["TOP_SAND deepest 1590","BASE_SAND crest 1570","BASE_SAND deepest 1615","TOP_SAND crest 1539.7181396484375"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The other three land exactly on picks: 1590 and 1615 are the Ekene-4 top and base, and 1570 is the Ekene-3 base. The TOP_SAND crest is the only mapped extreme that no well produced, which is why the maximum oil column carries an interpolation artefact.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 8, 'The oil sits at the top of the structure, so why is BASE_SAND gridded at all?', '["Because net to gross is obtained by dividing the net sand by the gross interval between the two surfaces","Because without it there is no way to know where the sand runs out beneath the accumulation","Because the base surface sets the 800 m extrapolation limit that produces the 201 live nodes in the mask","Because the oil column is measured from the base upwards to the contact"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sand has a floor, and a deep enough contact would let the accumulation fill to that floor, at which point the base rather than the contact limits the column. Net to gross arrives at this tier as a constant of 0.8 and is not derived from the surfaces.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 9, 'Treating the Ekene oil water contact as a flat plane at 1560 m is a teaching simplification. Which real behaviour does it leave out?', '["That porosity falls with depth across the accumulation, so a single value of 0.20 cannot describe the crest cells and the cells that sit just above the contact","That the extrapolation limit should be reduced near the contact","That capillary forces hold water above the free water level, so saturation grades through a transition zone","That the formation volume factor rises with the depth of the contact, so the constant 1.2 would not hold for a deeper booking"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Saturation grades through that transition zone rather than switching from oil to water at one depth. Real contacts also tilt under aquifer flow and can differ between fault blocks that are not in pressure communication, and none of that is in the Associate fixture. Porosity variation and the formation volume factor are property questions rather than contact geometry.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 10, 'Pressure measurements taken at intervals down a well give a lighter gradient in the oil leg and a steeper one in the water leg. What does the depth at which the two gradients cross provide?', '["An inferred contact depth, which is evidence to be judged rather than a contact that was measured","A direct measurement of the oil water contact at that well","The gross rock volume above the contact at that well","The sand thickness between TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nobody measures a contact directly. Logs, pressure gradients, fluid samples and seismic responses are all fragments of evidence, and the contact written on the map is a judgement about them that the next well can revise.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 11, 'How many of the six Ekene wells hold oil at the 1560 m contact?', '["All six, since every well carries both a TOP_SAND and a BASE_SAND pick","Four","Five","Two"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-1, Ekene-3, Ekene-5 and Ekene-6 have oil columns of 12, 19, 8 and 14 m, while Ekene-2 and Ekene-4 have their tops at 1565 m and 1590 m, both below the contact. Carrying a pick on both surfaces says the rock is there, not that it holds oil.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 12, 'Which well has the tallest oil column at the 1560 m contact, and how tall is it?', '["Ekene-2, with 36 m","Ekene-6, with 34 m","Ekene-3, with 19 m","Ekene-1, with 12 m"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-3 tops at 1541 m, the shallowest pick in the dataset, giving a 19 m column against the contact. The 36 m and 34 m figures are the sand thicknesses at Ekene-2 and Ekene-6, and quoting either as an oil column is the isochore confusion.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 13, 'In the Ekene chain at the 1560 m contact, the pore volume of 3.563046 million m3 is produced by which operation?', '["Net volume 17.815229 million m3 multiplied by porosity 0.20","Gross rock volume 22.269036 million m3 multiplied by porosity 0.20","Net volume 17.815229 million m3 multiplied by one minus the water saturation of 0.35","Gross rock volume 22.269036 million m3 multiplied by net to gross 0.8"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Porosity is applied to net rock rather than to gross rock, because net to gross has already removed the part of the interval that is not reservoir. Applying porosity straight to gross rock volume skips the net to gross step and overstates the pore volume.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 14, 'Why is the hydrocarbon pore volume divided by the formation volume factor of 1.2?', '["To remove the water that shares the pore space, since the water saturation of 0.35 is still carried inside the hydrocarbon pore volume","To convert cubic metres into stock tank barrels","To account for the shrinkage as oil is brought to the surface and dissolved gas comes out of solution","To correct the gross rock volume for the part of the interval that is not reservoir"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The formation volume factor is reservoir volume per stock tank volume, so dividing by 1.2 brings a reservoir condition volume to surface conditions. Water was already removed by the saturation step and the unit conversion is the separate factor of 6.2898.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-from-map-to-volume', 15, 'Steps 5 through 8 of the volumetrics workflow apply net to gross, porosity, water saturation and the formation volume factor. What distinguishes steps 1 through 4 from them?', '["Steps 1 through 4 are performed by the laboratory rather than by the interpreter","Steps 1 through 4 involve two surfaces, a contact and a node by node test, so their output is not a straight proportion of its inputs","Steps 1 through 4 are the only ones that can be checked against well control","Steps 1 through 4 are applied only to the 169 nodes that hold oil at the contact, while the later four steps are applied to all 201 live nodes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each of the later steps is a multiplication by a constant, so a fractional change in any of them moves STOIIP by the same fraction. The geometry is the part of the calculation whose response cannot be read off by inspection, which is why it carries most of the course.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 1, 'What is the clipping rule that gives the oil column at a node?', '["base minus top, discarding any node where the top is below the contact","the minimum of base and contact, minus top","the maximum of base and contact, minus top","contact minus base, wherever the base is deeper than the contact"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Oil fills the sand from its top down to whichever comes first, the floor of the rock or the water, and whichever comes first is the shallower of the two. Taking base minus top gives the sand thickness instead, which counts the water leg as oil.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 2, 'Why does the clipping rule take the minimum of the base and the contact rather than the maximum?', '["Because the minimum protects the calculation from null nodes on either surface","Because the base surface is always the shallower of the two on this field","Because the deeper of the two is the one that limits how far the oil can extend downwards","Because the oil stops at whichever of the two it meets first going down, and that is the shallower of them"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Two things can stop the column: the sand runs out, or the water starts. The shallower of the base and the contact is whichever the oil meets first, so the minimum is what the physical picture requires.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 3, 'A node has TOP_SAND below the contact, so the clipped column comes out as a negative number. What does that node contribute to the gross rock volume?', '["Zero, because the node is discarded before the summation","A negative contribution equal to the amount by which its top lies below the contact","The full sand thickness at that node, since the sand is still present","The contact depth minus the base, taken as a positive value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A node whose clipped column is zero or less holds no oil, and there is no such thing as negative oil, so it is skipped entirely. Letting the negative through is the classic beginner error at this step.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 4, 'What would go wrong if negative clipped columns were allowed into the sum instead of being discarded?', '["The oil-bearing cell count would rise from 169 towards the 201 live nodes, because the 32 dry nodes would enter the sum","The maximum oil column would be reported from the wrong node","Deep ground with no oil would cancel real rock elsewhere, so the gross rock volume would come out too small","The two surfaces would stop sharing one mask, so the live node count would change"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A signed sum lets ground far below the contact subtract rock that genuinely exists elsewhere, biasing the gross rock volume low, and the bias deepens the more downdip ground the map covers. Nothing about the resulting number looks wrong on the page, which is what makes the error dangerous.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 5, 'The contact is revised from 1560 m to a shallower depth. Which quantity is unaffected by that revision?', '["The oil column at each well","The sand thickness at each well","The number of oil-bearing cells","The gross rock volume"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sand thickness is base minus top, a property of the rock that no fluid enters, so it is the same in a dry hole as in the best producer. The oil column, the cell count and the volume all move with the contact.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 6, 'Someone takes a map of sand thickness, multiplies it by area and by the property constants, and calls the result the oil in place. What have they actually computed?', '["The volume of the whole sand body, water leg included","The oil in place, but biased low because the crestal cells were left out","The gross rock volume above the contact, which still needs the property constants applied","The hydrocarbon pore volume, which still needs the formation volume factor applied"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An isochore map has no contact in it, so the result is a correct estimate of a different quantity, which is worse than a visible mistake because it is internally consistent. It counts the water leg under every producing well and the whole section at both dry wells, all 36 m at Ekene-2 and all 25 m at Ekene-4.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 7, 'The Ekene map carries two counts at the 1560 m contact: 201 and 169. What does each depend on?', '["201 depends on the contact you assume, while 169 depends on the well positions together with the 800 m extrapolation limit","Both depend only on the cell size of 100 m","Both depend on the contact, since a node with no oil is not mapped","201 depends on the well positions and the 800 m limit, 169 depends on those and on the contact"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The live count says how much ground the map is entitled to describe, and the oil-bearing count says how much of that ground holds oil at the contact you assumed. The 32 nodes in between are fully mapped ground where the sand lies entirely beneath the water.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 8, 'With 169 oil-bearing cells and each cell covering 10,000 square metres, what is the oil area at the 1560 m contact?', '["0.169 km2","16.9 km2","1.69 km2","About 2 square kilometres, which is the ground the map covers"]'::jsonb, 2, '169 multiplied by 10,000 is 1,690,000 square metres, which is 1.69 km2. Quoting the whole mapped area instead is the error of reporting the live node footprint as though the entire map held oil.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 9, 'Why is the oil area a better thing to quote in a report than the oil-bearing cell count?', '["Because area is measured in the field while a cell count is computed","Because the cell count changes with the cell size you happened to choose, while the area does not","Because area already includes the net to gross correction","Because the cell count drops from 169 to 128 when the contact moves up to 1550 m, while the area does not move"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Regrid the same field at a finer cell and the count rises while the ground it stands for lands in the same place. Both numbers depend on the contact, so that is not what separates them.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 10, 'How much does the BASE_SAND surface contribute to the Ekene booking at the 1560 m contact?', '["Nothing, because BASE_SAND is deeper than the contact at all 169 oil-bearing nodes","It limits the column at the crestal cells, where the sand runs out before the contact is reached","It sets the floor of the accumulation across most of the field, with the contact limiting only the flanks","It fixes the shared mask of 201 live nodes, and with it the count of 169 oil-bearing cells"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every oil-bearing node on Ekene at this contact is contact limited and none is base limited, so the column reduces to the contact minus the top everywhere and deleting the base surface would not change the volume by a barrel. Assuming the base limits the crest is a reasonable guess that this fixture does not support.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 11, 'At what contact depth would the Ekene accumulation begin to be base limited at some of its cells?', '["At 1560 m, the capstone contact","At 1539.7181396484375 m, the mapped crest of TOP_SAND","At 1615 m, the deepest mapped value on BASE_SAND","Below 1570 m, the shallowest mapped value on BASE_SAND"]'::jsonb, 3, 'While the contact stays shallower than every mapped base value, the minimum in the clipping rule always returns the contact. Once the contact passes 1570 m the crestal cells fill to the floor of the sand and stop deepening.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 12, 'The maximum oil column at the capstone contact is 20.2818603515625 m. Which difference produces it?', '["The mapped crest of BASE_SAND minus the mapped crest of TOP_SAND","The contact at 1560 m minus the shallowest pick of 1541 m at Ekene-3","The contact at 1560 m minus the mapped crest of 1539.7181396484375 m","The mean oil column of 13.176944 m subtracted from the sand thickness at Ekene-6"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every oil-bearing node is contact limited, so the tallest column sits at the shallowest mapped node and equals the contact minus the mapped crest. Using the shallowest pick instead would give a different and smaller figure, which is exactly the gap this course asks you to report.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 13, 'Which statement about the maximum oil column of 20.2818603515625 m is correct?', '["It is measured down from a mapped value that no well produced","It is the oil column measured at Ekene-3, the shallowest well in the field","It is the thickest sand in the field, the 36 m of section at Ekene-2","It is the average column over the 169 oil-bearing cells, which is 13.176944 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mapped crest is the thin-plate spline overshooting past its last control, landing 1.2819 m shallower than the shallowest pick, so the headline column inherits an interpolation artefact one for one. Reading it as a value measured at a well is the mistake this lesson exists to prevent.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 14, 'The mean oil column over the accumulation is 13.176944 m and the maximum is 20.2818603515625 m. What follows from that comparison?', '["The distribution of columns is centred on its maximum, so either figure characterises the field","Multiplying the maximum column by the oil area would overstate the rock","The mean is the value that should be quoted as the capstone answer for maximum oil column","The difference between the two shows that some cells are base limited"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A maximum is a single value read at a single node, and on Ekene that node is the one the spline was most free to invent. An accumulation is described by its area with a mean column, or by the gross rock volume itself.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-oil-column', 15, 'Where do the 32 mapped nodes that hold no oil at the 1560 m contact sit?', '["Around the crest near Ekene-1 and Ekene-3, where the top surface is shallowest and the columns are tallest","Scattered evenly across the mapped area","Along the western edge near Ekene-5, where the grid reaches the 800 m extrapolation limit and stops","In the eastern strip of the map, around Ekene-2 and Ekene-4, where the top surface is deepest"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A node holds no oil when its TOP_SAND value lies below the contact, so the dry ground is the deepest ground, running from about x 2100 to 2600 m and y 1200 to 2500 m. Ekene-2 at 1565 m and Ekene-4 at 1590 m are the two dry wells and they sit in that strip.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 1, 'The Ekene grid uses a 100 m cell. How much ground does one oil bearing cell stand for in the gross rock volume sum?', '["100 square metres","169 square metres","10,000 square metres","1,690,000 square metres"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A cell is 100 m by 100 m, so its area is 10,000 square metres. The figure 1,690,000 square metres is the area of the whole accumulation, which is 169 cells at 10,000 square metres each, and 100 is the cell side rather than its area.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 2, 'Which statement describes how the engine arrives at gross rock volume?', '["It sums the oil column at each cell multiplied by the cell area","It multiplies the mapped area by the maximum oil column","It multiplies the mapped area by the thickness measured at the nearest well","It integrates the top surface analytically and subtracts the contact"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gross rock volume is a sum of one box per cell, each 100 m by 100 m in plan and as tall as the column at that node. Using the maximum column would treat every cell as though it were at the crest, which overstates the volume badly.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 3, 'You divide the gross rock volume at the capstone contact by the accumulation area of 1,690,000 square metres. What do you get, and what is the check for?', '["13.176944 m, the maximum oil column, which must match the crest","13.176944 m, the mean oil column, which must be plausible as a thickness","22.269036 m, the mean oil column, which must match the gross rock volume","1.69 m, the mean oil column, which must match the mapped area"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dividing a volume that is the sum of column times a constant area by the total area recovers the mean column, here 13.176944 m. The value of the check is that a mean thickness has a range a person can judge, so a unit slip shows up at once.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 4, 'What does a net to gross of 0.8 state about the interval between TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND?', '["Four fifths of the pore space holds hydrocarbon, leaving one fifth of it as connate water","Four fifths of the oil in place can be recovered to the stock tank","Four fifths of the mapped nodes carry a depth value on both surfaces","Four fifths of the gross interval is reservoir quality rock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Net to gross is the fraction of the gross interval that passes the reservoir cutoffs. The fraction of pore space holding hydrocarbon is the saturation term, and the recoverable fraction is the recovery factor, which never enters a volumetric chain.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 5, 'Working in the six decimal chain form, gross rock volume 22.269036 million cubic metres multiplied by a net to gross of 0.8 gives which net rock volume?', '["3.563046 million cubic metres","17.815229 million cubic metres","2.315980 million cubic metres","22.269036 million cubic metres"]'::jsonb, 1, '22.269036 times 0.8 is 17.815229 million cubic metres. The figures 3.563046 and 2.315980 are the pore volume and the hydrocarbon pore volume, which are two and three steps further down the chain.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 6, 'A property sheet reports net to gross 0.8, gross average porosity 0.20, and a quantity called net average porosity of 0.16. What has already happened to 0.16?', '["The net to gross factor has already been folded into it","The water saturation of 0.35 has already been removed from it","The formation volume factor has already been applied to it","It has already been converted to stock tank conditions by the 1.2 factor"]'::jsonb, 0, '0.16 is 0.20 multiplied by 0.8, so the sheet has folded net to gross into the porosity. Applying net to gross to the gross rock volume and then using 0.16 applies the same factor twice, which understates the booking and leaves every step looking plausible.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 7, 'Working in the six decimal chain form, net rock volume 17.815229 million cubic metres multiplied by a porosity of 0.20 gives which pore volume?', '["17.815229 million cubic metres","22.269036 million cubic metres","0.731816 million cubic metres","3.563046 million cubic metres"]'::jsonb, 3, '17.815229 times 0.20 is 3.563046 million cubic metres, which the engine reports at full precision as the graded 3.563045809312045. The figure 0.731816 is the hydrocarbon pore volume at a contact of 1550 m.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 8, 'The porosity of 0.20 used in the chain is the porosity of which rock?', '["The net reservoir quality rock only","The gross interval including its shales and cemented streaks","The pore space above the contact","The hydrocarbon filled part of the pore space"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Porosity here is the fraction of the net rock that is void, which is why it multiplies the net rock volume. Applying it to the gross rock volume instead credits the shales with reservoir porosity and overstates the pore volume.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 9, 'Which pairing of a volume with a porosity gives the same pore volume as net rock volume with 0.20?', '["Gross rock volume with a net average porosity of 0.20 read from the property sheet","Net rock volume with a gross average porosity of 0.16 read from the property sheet","Gross rock volume with a gross average porosity of 0.16","Hydrocarbon pore volume with a gross average porosity of 0.16"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both consistent routes apply the same two fractions once each, so gross rock volume times 0.16 matches net rock volume times 0.20. Mixing gross volume with net porosity omits the net to gross step, and net volume with gross porosity applies the shale dilution twice.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 10, 'Working in the six decimal chain form, pore volume 3.563046 million cubic metres with a water saturation of 0.35 gives which hydrocarbon pore volume?', '["3.563046 million cubic metres","2.315980 million cubic metres","17.815229 million cubic metres","0.731816 million cubic metres"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The factor is 1 minus the water saturation, which is 0.65, and 3.563046 times 0.65 is 2.315980 million cubic metres. The engine reports it at full precision as the graded 2.3159797972902343.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 11, 'A colleague multiplies the pore volume by 0.35 rather than by 0.65. What have they computed?', '["The hydrocarbon pore volume, expressed at stock tank conditions rather than at reservoir conditions","The net rock volume, with the net to gross factor of 0.8 applied twice","The volume of connate water, which they are about to book as oil","The recoverable oil volume, since a recovery factor of 0.35 has been applied"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Multiplying by the water saturation itself returns the water in the pore system rather than the oil. This is the most common arithmetic error in volumetrics, and at a water saturation of 0.5 the two results are identical, so it can pass unnoticed.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 12, 'Why does the rock above the oil water contact still hold water?', '["Capillary forces hold a film of connate water on the grains and in the smallest pore throats","The contact was mapped too shallow, so some water bearing cells were included","Water is produced along with the oil and is counted in the pore volume","The net to gross fraction of 0.8 leaves one fifth of the interval as shale, and shale holds water"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Migrating oil cannot displace the water held by capillary forces on the grain surfaces and in the finest pore throats, so a connate water saturation remains everywhere above the contact. The contact removed the fully water saturated cells, and the 1 minus water saturation term removes this second, immobile water.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 13, 'Why does converting the hydrocarbon pore volume to a stock tank volume mean dividing by the formation volume factor?', '["Because the factor is stock tank volume per reservoir volume, and the oil expands on the way up","Because the factor converts cubic metres to barrels and barrels are smaller","Because the factor is reservoir volume per stock tank volume, and the oil shrinks on the way up","Because the factor removes the connate water that the saturation step left behind"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The formation volume factor is defined as reservoir volume divided by stock tank volume and is greater than one for any oil with gas in solution, so dividing by it makes the answer smaller, which is the direction shrinkage requires. Multiplying by it instead invents oil that does not exist.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 14, 'The hydrocarbon pore volume is 2.3159797972902343 million cubic metres, the formation volume factor is 1.2, and one cubic metre is 6.2898 stock tank barrels. What is the booked stock tank oil initially in place?', '["2.3159797972902343 MMstb","12.139208107496763 MMstb","3.835815 MMstb","22.044451 MMstb"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dividing by 1.2 and multiplying by 6.2898 gives 12.139208107496763 MMstb, which is the graded figure. The values 3.835815 and 22.044451 are the bookings at contacts of 1550 m and 1570 m.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-volumetric-chain', 15, 'What does the word initially do in the name stock tank oil initially in place?', '["It marks the figure as a first pass that a later study will replace","It states that only the first year of production is counted","It signals that the recovery factor has yet to be applied","It fixes the volume at discovery, before any oil is produced"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The volume is the oil in the ground at the moment of discovery, so it does not decline as the field is produced. The separate limitation is in place, which is why quoting this number as reserves rather than multiplying by a recovery factor is a category error.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 1, 'Porosity in the chain is computed from which measurement, and which input is a choice rather than a measurement?', '["The bulk density log, with the assumed matrix density as the choice","The deep resistivity log, with the assumed matrix density as the choice","The gamma ray log, with the assumed clean sand and shale baselines as the choice","A laboratory depletion study, with the sample quality as the choice"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The density porosity relation converts the logged bulk density using an assumed matrix density and fluid density, both of which are choices. Assume a sandstone matrix where the rock is dolomite and every porosity in the well is wrong.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 2, 'Water saturation is computed from the deep resistivity log through Archie. Which statement about that calculation is correct?', '["It is independent of porosity, so porosity errors cannot reach it","It is measured directly by the deep resistivity tool, so no other log enters the saturation","It depends on porosity, so a porosity error propagates into saturation","It is derived from the gamma ray once shale volume is known"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Archie takes porosity as an input alongside the measured deep resistivity, the formation water resistivity and the exponents, so porosity error enters saturation and then enters the chain a second time. Archie also assumes a clean water wet formation, which is why shaly sand models exist.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 3, 'Which statement about a net to gross of 0.8 is correct?', '["No tool measured it; it follows from the cutoffs that were chosen","It is read directly from the density log over the gross interval","It is measured on core and is a property of the rock rather than a decision","It is derived from the formation volume factor and the porosity, both of which come from the laboratory"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Net to gross is a counting result: samples that pass the shale volume, porosity and where relevant saturation cutoffs are net, and the ratio of net to gross thickness follows. A different but equally defensible set of cutoffs gives a different figure, so the useful question is which cutoffs produced it.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 4, 'Which of the four properties in the chain is not a petrophysical result computed from logs?', '["Net to gross","Porosity","The formation volume factor","Water saturation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The formation volume factor is a fluid property from a pressure, volume and temperature study on a sample, or from a correlation where no sample exists. It also depends on pressure, so the value used in a volumetric is the one at initial reservoir conditions.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 5, 'The Associate tier uses one porosity everywhere. What does the engine that produced the module 3 numbers actually take as its porosity input?', '["A single scalar that it applies once to the summed pore volume after the node loop has finished","An array with one value per grid node, filled with the same number at every node","A value read from the nearest of the six wells for each cell, so porosity steps at the boundaries between well areas","A trend surface fitted to the six well porosities, which is the input the Expert tier uses at this same contact"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine always works cell by cell on per node arrays, and the Associate tier hands it a flat surface. That is why moving to varying properties needs different inputs rather than a different calculation.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 6, 'Why does replacing a constant porosity with a property grid change the booked volume even when the average of the grid equals the constant?', '["Because a gridded porosity always sits above the plain average of the six well values once it is spread over 201 nodes","Because the total becomes a sum of per cell products, and a sum of products is not a product of sums","Because gridding changes the number of cells that fall in the accumulation, so the 169 oil cells are recounted","Because a grid forces the net to gross of 0.8 to be applied a second time, once on the grid and once in the chain"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With a constant the total separates into volume times fraction, and with a grid each cell contributes its own product before the sum. The two agree only when the property is uncorrelated with the column.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 7, 'Structural highs often carry cleaner, better sorted rock. If porosity is higher where the oil column is tallest, what does using the plain arithmetic average of the well porosities do to the booking?', '["It overstates it, because the thinnest cells carry the best rock","It leaves it unchanged, because averaging is exact in a product","It understates it, because averaging removes the net to gross factor","It understates it, because the thickest cells carry the best rock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'When the property correlates positively with column height, the volume weighted average sits above the plain average of the wells, so the plain average understates the booking. Where the correlation runs the other way the error runs the other way.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 8, 'The Expert tier reruns the chain at the same contact with a fitted porosity trend and books 12.796077 MMstb against the 12.139208 MMstb the constant produced, a difference of 0.656868 MMstb. Why does subtracting the two printed figures not give exactly that difference?', '["The difference is computed from the unrounded engine values, so it differs in the last digit","The two bookings were made at different contacts, 1560 m for one and 1570 m for the other, so they are not comparable","The trend booking has already had the formation volume factor of 1.2 applied twice, once by the engine and once on the page","The printed figures are in different units, so the subtraction is invalid"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both bookings are correct at the precision they are quoted to, and the delta comes from the unrounded values, which is why the last digit differs from a subtraction done on the page. The engine difference is the one to trust, and none of the three figures should be carried into an Associate answer.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 9, 'Every property enters the chain exactly once as a factor. What follows for a relative error in net to gross or in porosity?', '["The booked volume changes by half that relative amount","The booked volume changes by the square of that relative amount","The booked volume changes by the same relative amount, one for one","The booked volume is unaffected until the error exceeds a cutoff"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In a product, a relative change in one factor produces the same relative change in the result, and here it holds exactly rather than approximately. Net to gross and porosity therefore have identical leverage, which is worth remembering because porosity gets far more attention in reviews.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 10, 'A relative error in water saturation moves the booking by less than the same relative error in porosity. Why?', '["The chain uses the water saturation directly, so the effect is damped by the porosity","Water saturation enters the chain twice and the two effects partly cancel","The chain uses 1 minus the water saturation, so the effect is damped","Water saturation is dimensionless, so relative errors in it do not propagate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A 10 percent relative error takes a water saturation of 0.35 to 0.385, so the hydrocarbon fraction moves from 0.65 to 0.615, a change of 0.035 out of 0.65. The damping factor is the ratio of water saturation to hydrocarbon fraction, which here is 0.35 over 0.65.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 11, 'The laboratory revises the formation volume factor upward and nothing else changes. What happens to the booked volume?', '["It rises, because the chain multiplies by the formation volume factor","It falls, because a higher factor implies a higher water saturation","It is unchanged, because the factor cancels against the barrel conversion","It falls, because the chain divides by the formation volume factor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A higher factor means more shrinkage between reservoir and tank, and since the chain divides by it the booking falls. The response is inverse in sign and only very slightly damped in size.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 12, 'Moving the contact from 1550 m to 1560 m and then to 1570 m takes the cell count from 128 to 169 to 190. Why is the volume response steeper than any property effect can be?', '["The contact changes the net to gross and the porosity at the same time, since both are cut off at the oil water contact","The contact is applied twice, once to the top surface and once to the base","The contact changes the cell size, so each cell covers more ground","The contact adds area and adds column to every cell already contributing, so two effects compound"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A property multiplies a fixed volume by a fixed fraction and can only produce a proportional change, while the contact changes which cells are in the accumulation at all and lengthens the column on the rest. That is why the contact should be established before the properties are argued about.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 13, 'Oil in place is reported as 12.139208 MMstb. What does the MM prefix mean?', '["Two million, since the M is written twice","A million, since M is the Roman numeral for a thousand","A thousand, since M is the metric prefix for a thousand","A billion, since MM is the oilfield prefix for a thousand million"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The convention comes from Roman numerals, in which M is one thousand, so MM is a million. That is why gas rates appear as Mscf for thousands of standard cubic feet and MMscf for millions.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 14, 'You have a volume in cubic metres and need it in stock tank barrels. What do you do, and what is the quick check?', '["Multiply by 0.159, and check that the barrel count is the smaller number","Multiply by 6.2898, and check that the barrel count is the larger number","Divide by 6.2898, and check that the barrel count is the smaller number","Divide by 0.159, and check that the barrel count is the smaller number"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A barrel is smaller than a cubic metre, so it takes more of them to hold the same oil and the conversion multiplies. Using 0.159 is the reciprocal and takes you the other way, which produces an answer wrong by a factor of 6.2898 squared.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-properties', 15, 'Which unit error is the most dangerous in a volumetric report?', '["A factor of 6.2898, because the result still looks like a plausible field","A factor of 10 to the sixth, because the result is absurd and easy to spot","A factor of 10 to the sixth, because it changes the shape of the map","A missing decimal point, because it changes the mean column"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A booking six times too small or too large survives a quick glance, while a dropped or added million prefix produces a field holding twelve barrels or twelve trillion, which nobody will discuss for long. The defence is to carry the unit through every line rather than only the last.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 1, 'At the Associate tier the booked STOIIP depends on the mapped rock, four constant properties and the depth of the oil water contact. Which of those inputs has by far the largest effect on the booked volume?', '["The net to gross of 0.8","The depth of the oil water contact","The formation volume factor of 1.2","The porosity of 0.20"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Holding the map and the properties fixed, contacts of 1550, 1560 and 1570 m book 3.835815, 12.139208 and 22.044451 MMstb. Each property enters the chain as a single multiplication by a constant, so none of them can move the answer anything like that far.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 2, 'The same Ekene map and the same properties are used at three contacts, 1550, 1560 and 1570 m. What are the three STOIIP figures, in that order?', '["12.139208, 22.044451 and 40.439926 MMstb","3.835815, 7.036696 and 12.139208 MMstb","3.835815, 12.139208 and 22.044451 MMstb","7.036696, 22.269036 and 40.439926 MMstb"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The booking roughly triples and then roughly doubles across the two 10 m steps. The set 7.036696, 22.269036 and 40.439926 is the gross rock volume column in million m3, and reading the GRV column where the STOIIP column belongs is a common slip.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 3, 'The contact does two separate jobs in the volume calculation. What are they?', '["It decides which mapped nodes hold oil at all, and it sets the oil column at every node that does","It sets the porosity and the water saturation used at each node","It fixes the cell size of the grid and the extrapolation limit","It fixes the mapped crest of 1539.7181396484375 m and the deepest mapped value of 1590 m on the top surface"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 1560 m, 169 of the 201 mapped nodes have TOP_SAND above the contact and the other 32 do not, and at each of the 169 the column is the contact minus the mapped top. The grid settings and the properties are decided before the contact is applied and do not respond to it.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 4, 'At a contact of 1560 m, which two Ekene wells are dry, and why?', '["Ekene-1 and Ekene-5, because their columns of 12 m and 8 m are too thin to book","Ekene-3 and Ekene-6, because their tops sit above the contact","Ekene-2 and Ekene-5, because they fall outside the 800 m extrapolation limit","Ekene-2 and Ekene-4, because their tops lie below the contact"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A well is dry at a contact when its top is deeper than that contact, and the Ekene-2 and Ekene-4 tops sit at 1565 m and 1590 m. The other four wells carry columns of 12, 19, 8 and 14 m, each of them the contact minus that well''s own pick.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 5, 'Why is the STOIIP response to the contact much steeper than linear?', '["Because porosity increases with depth in this fixture","Because a deeper contact adds oil area and oil column at the same time, and the two factors multiply","Because the BASE_SAND surface starts adding rock volume once the contact passes it","Because the thin plate spline bends more sharply at deeper contacts"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Gross rock volume is roughly the cell count times the mean column times the cell area. The count goes 128, 169 and 190 while the maximum column goes 10.2818603515625, 20.2818603515625 and 30.2818603515625 m, so both terms rise together and their product rises faster than either.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 6, 'The oil cell count rises from 128 to 169 and then only to 190. What limits the growth of the area term on this map?', '["The 800 m extrapolation limit is recomputed at each contact, so the mask shrinks as the accumulation spreads outward","The maximum oil column stops growing once it passes 20.2818603515625 m, the value at the capstone contact","The map carries only 201 live nodes in total, so the count cannot pass that ceiling","The BASE_SAND surface removes cells as the contact deepens, since its crest of 1570 m caps the column there"]'::jsonb, 2, 'By 1570 m, 190 of the 201 available nodes already hold oil, so there is very little ground left to gain. On a real field the same ceiling exists, set by a spill point or by the limit of mapped ground rather than by a mask.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 7, 'Why does the maximum oil column rise by exactly 10 m for each 10 m the contact is deepened, giving 10.2818603515625, 20.2818603515625 and 30.2818603515625 m?', '["Because the maximum column is the contact minus the mapped crest","Because the cell size is 100 m and the maximum column is averaged over the ten cells nearest the crest","Because the mean oil column of 13.176944 m rises by 10 m at each step","Because the BASE_SAND crest of 1570 m fixes the increment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mapped TOP_SAND crest of 1539.7181396484375 m is a property of the surface rather than of the contact, so every metre of contact is a metre of maximum column. The mean column and the maximum column are different quantities and only the maximum has this exact relationship.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 8, 'At the capstone contact, why does deepening the contact by one metre add a metre of column at every one of the 169 oil cells rather than at only some of them?', '["Because all 169 cells carry the same oil column","Because the cell area of 10,000 square metres is the same everywhere","Because the net to gross of 0.8 is applied uniformly, so every cell gains the same net thickness","Because the BASE_SAND surface is deeper than the contact at all 169 cells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The accumulation is contact limited at 1560 m, so at each of the 169 cells the column is the contact minus the mapped top and nothing caps it from below. That regime holds until the contact reaches the shallowest point on BASE_SAND at 1570 m, below which the base begins to cap columns.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 9, 'You know the STOIIP at 1550 m and at 1570 m and you need the answer at 1560 m. What should you do?', '["Average the two, since the contact steps are equal","Recompute at 1560 m, because the response is not linear","Take the deeper case, since a booking should lean optimistic","Take the shallower case, since the low case is always the safe one to quote"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two ends are 3.835815 and 22.044451 MMstb while the true mid case is 12.139208 MMstb, which is below their average. Treating the contact response as linear is one of the commonest beginner errors.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 10, 'Where does the 1560 m contact used in this course come from?', '["From a pressure gradient analysis on the six Ekene wells, where the oil line and the water line cross at 1560 m","From a fluid contact logged in Ekene-2, whose TOP_SAND pick of 1565 m sits just below it","It is assumed, since the Ekene wells carry only TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND picks","From the deepest oil column in the field, the 19 m at Ekene-3, taken as reaching the contact"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The wells carry two picks each and nothing else, so the contact was chosen to show what a contact does. An assumed contact is a legitimate input, and quoting it as though it were a measurement is not.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 11, 'At the 1560 m contact the four oil wells show columns of 12, 19, 8 and 14 m. How should those columns be treated as evidence about the contact?', '["They are not evidence, since each was produced by subtracting that well''s top from the assumed contact","They confirm the contact, since four wells agree with it independently","They put a lower bound on the contact at 1560 m","They show the contact must be deeper than 1560 m, since Ekene-3 carries 19 m of oil"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The columns are consequences of the assumption rather than support for it. Circular support of this kind is common in real projects, where a contact set years ago is later quoted as though it were data.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 12, 'A pressure gradient analysis places the free water level where the oil line and the water line intersect. What most often limits its accuracy?', '["It cannot be run unless the gauge resolves the density difference between the fluids directly","It requires a well that drilled through the contact itself","It cannot be used unless a seismic flat spot is present as well","The crossing depth is only as good as the two fitted slopes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each line needs points only in its own fluid, so the method works without a well through the contact. A slope fitted to few points spread over a short interval can swing the crossing depth by metres, which is why a gradient result should be reported with the depth range of the points that constrained each line.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 13, 'Which set of information has to accompany a booked STOIIP for it to count as a result?', '["The number of wells, the operator and the date of the booking","The contact assumed, the properties assumed, the surface and its grid settings, and the range across contact cases","The porosity, the water saturation and the number of decimal places used, so the reader can repeat the arithmetic exactly","The mapped crest, the deepest mapped value and the contour interval"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Without those four a reader can neither reproduce the number nor disagree with it in a specific place. Reporting a single STOIIP with no contact stated is the classic beginner failure.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 14, 'What is wrong with sending a partner the figure 12.139208107496763 MMstb on its own?', '["The value is arithmetically wrong at that precision, since the chain was built from figures quoted to six decimals","It should have been converted to cubic metres first, since the chain works in cubic metres until the final step","It claims a precision the inputs do not support and says nothing about the contact it assumes","It is the pore volume of 3.563046 million m3 brought to the surface rather than the stock tank oil in place"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every digit is what the engine returned, so the arithmetic is not the problem. A contact uncertain by ten metres cannot support a volume quoted to a fraction of a barrel, and the capstone is the one audience that wants the engine value.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-contact', 15, 'A colleague quotes a porosity well constrained by six core plugs and a good log suite, and books 22.044451 MMstb. What is the first thing to establish?', '["Whether the core plugs were measured at reservoir conditions","Whether the net to gross came from the same log suite","Whether the barrels are stock tank or reservoir barrels","Which contact the booking assumes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'That figure is the 1570 m case, and the same map and properties give 12.139208 MMstb at 1560 m and 3.835815 MMstb at 1550 m. Confidence in the properties tells you very little about confidence in the volume.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 1, 'What is the working order of the volumetrics workflow at this tier?', '["Picks, surfaces, contact, columns, rock volume, properties, report","Contact, picks, properties, surfaces, columns, rock volume, report","Surfaces, picks, rock volume, contact, columns, properties, report","Picks, contact, properties, columns, surfaces, rock volume, report"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each step consumes what the step before it produced. The contact cannot be clipped until both surfaces exist, and the properties cannot be applied until there is a rock volume to apply them to.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 2, 'Which step of the workflow adds no information to the volume and yet controls all of it?', '["The choice of a 100 m cell size, since it sets the 500 frame nodes the volume is summed over","The picks, since everything downstream is arithmetic on them","The 800 m extrapolation limit, since it decides which 201 of the 500 frame nodes are live","The conversion of cubic metres to barrels at 6.2898 stb per m3"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A top picked ten metres low is ten metres low in the map and in every oil column the map produces. Nothing downstream of the correlation can detect it, which is why the picks control the booking without contributing any arithmetic of their own.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 3, 'What did the mapping step contribute to the Ekene booking?', '["The four constant properties used in the chain","The 1560 m contact and the oil column at each of the six wells, since the mapping step is where both of those are set","Two surfaces on one frame, with 201 live nodes out of 500 at a 100 m cell after masking at 800 m","The 169 oil cells and the maximum oil column of 20.2818603515625 m, both read straight off the mapped surfaces"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The oil cell count and the maximum column come later, from clipping those surfaces against the contact. The mask is the reason the volume claims 1.69 square kilometres of ground rather than the whole frame.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 4, 'How do you check the gross rock volume a second way, without rerunning the engine?', '["Multiply the maximum oil column of 20.2818603515625 m by the oil area","Multiply the 201 live nodes by the cell area of 10,000 square metres","Divide the STOIIP of 12.139208 MMstb by 6.2898","Multiply the mean oil column of 13.176944 m by the oil area of 1,690,000 square metres"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 169 oil cells each stand for 10,000 square metres, giving 1.69 square kilometres, and the mean column over that area returns 22.269036 million m3. Using the maximum column in place of the mean overstates the rock volume, and using all 201 live nodes counts ground that holds no oil.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 5, 'Which check catches a booking where the porosity was applied twice?', '["Walking the chain link by link and confirming each is one multiplication by the expected constant","Comparing the dry wells against the contact","Confirming that the maximum column equals the contact at 1560 m minus the mapped crest of 1539.7181396484375 m","Confirming that the accumulation is contact limited"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pore volume must equal net volume times 0.20, so 17.815229 times 0.20 gives 3.563046. A property applied twice or left out is the commonest error in a spreadsheet version of this workflow, and only the chain reconstruction finds it.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 6, 'The maximum oil column at the capstone contact is 20.2818603515625 m. Why is that number described as carrying an artefact?', '["Because it was measured at Ekene-3, the shallowest well in the field, rather than read off the mapped surface","Because the mapped crest of 1539.7181396484375 m is 1.2819 m shallower than any well pick","Because the 100 m cell size rounds the column upward, since the crest falls between two nodes of the frame","Because the BASE_SAND surface caps the column at its crest of 1570 m rather than at the contact"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The column is the contact at 1560 m minus the mapped crest, and the thin plate spline bends over the high and carries that crest slightly above its highest pin, Ekene-3 at 1541 m. No rock has been seen at that depth, so the maximum column is a number to explain rather than a discovery.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 7, 'How do you tell whether the Ekene accumulation is contact limited or base limited at 1560 m, and what is the answer?', '["Compare the mean column against the maximum column; it is base limited because the two differ","Compare the oil cell count against the live node count; it is base limited because 169 is less than 201","Ask which surface cuts off the column at each cell; it is contact limited at all 169 oil cells","Ask whether the well columns match their picks; it is contact limited because two wells are dry"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The column is the shallower of the base and the contact, minus the top, so one of the two is always doing the cutting. At every one of the 169 oil cells BASE_SAND lies deeper than the contact, so the base contributes nothing here and would only begin to matter if the contact were pushed deeper than the shallowest point on BASE_SAND at 1570 m.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 8, 'Which statement about units in the chain is correct?', '["Bo is multiplied by 1.2, because it converts a stock tank volume into the larger reservoir volume the oil occupied in place","The factor 6.2898 converts barrels to cubic metres","Net to gross and porosity change the units of the volume as well as its size, so the pore volume is not in the same units as the gross rock volume","Bo is divided to convert reservoir volume to surface volume, and 6.2898 stb per m3 then converts cubic metres to barrels"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Net to gross, porosity and one minus Sw are dimensionless, so they change the size of the number and not its units. A Bo multiplied instead of divided, or a missing factor of a million, produces a figure that looks plausible in a table and is wrong by orders of magnitude.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 9, 'Which of these is not one of the six quantities the capstone grades?', '["The net rock volume of 17.815229 million m3","The pore volume","The maximum oil column","The oil-bearing grid cell count"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The capstone grades the oil-bearing cell count, the maximum oil column, the gross rock volume, the pore volume, the hydrocarbon pore volume and the STOIIP. Net rock volume is a real link in the chain and it is shown on the panel, and it is not graded.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 10, 'What tolerance does the capstone allow on the oil-bearing grid cell count?', '["0.05, the same as the gross rock volume","None, the tolerance is 0 and the count must be exact","0.01, the same as the pore volume","One cell either side, since the mask edge is approximate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A count carries no partial credit, so 168 and 170 are both wrong. Assuming that some tolerance exists on a count is a costly beginner assumption, and a count that is not 169 usually means the contact or the cell size is set wrongly.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 11, 'A learner enters 17.815229 in the pore volume field of the capstone. What has gone wrong?', '["They read the gross rock volume instead of the pore volume","They forgot to divide by the formation volume factor","They entered the ungraded net rock volume, which sits on the panel directly above the pore volume","They entered the value in plain cubic metres rather than in the units of 10^6 m3 that every volume on the capstone is graded in"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Net rock volume is the gross rock volume after net to gross, and the two lines sit next to each other on the panel. The graded pore volume is 3.563045809312045 in units of 10^6 m3, with a tolerance of 0.01.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 12, 'In which units does the capstone expect the gross rock volume, the pore volume and the hydrocarbon pore volume?', '["Cubic metres","Reservoir barrels","MMstb","10^6 m3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Units are graded as part of the value, and only the STOIIP is quoted in MMstb. Entering one of those volumes in plain cubic metres puts it a factor of a million away from the answer.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 13, 'How many decimal places do you need to enter to clear every tolerance on the capstone?', '["Three, since 22.269, 3.563, 2.316 and 12.139 all sit inside their windows","All of them, since the graded values are held at full precision","One, since every tolerance is at least 0.1","None, since the graded values are whole numbers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The long engine values state what is held rather than how you must enter it. The tightest windows are 0.01 on the pore volume and the hydrocarbon pore volume, so read those from the panel rather than rebuilding them from a heavily rounded gross rock volume.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 14, 'The Professional tier puts a sealing fault at an easting of 1800 m, giving 117 cells and 9.855617 MMstb in the west and 52 cells and 2.283591 MMstb in the east. What does that change?', '["The field STOIIP, which grows once the two blocks are summed","The development case, since the blocks do not drain to each other, while the field total is unchanged","The mapped surface, which has to be regridded on each side of the fault","The contact, which has to be set separately in each block before the 117 west cells and the 52 east cells can be summed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cells and the barrels both add back to the beginner figure of 12.139208 MMstb, and a partition that does not sum has a bug in it. Volumes add and decisions do not, since a block holding 2.283591 MMstb may not carry a well at all.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-volumetrics-workflow', 15, 'The Expert tier replaces the constant porosity of 0.20 with a trend fitted to the six wells and rebooks at the same contact. What happens?', '["The booking is unchanged, since the fitted trend averages back to the 0.20 constant over the 169 oil cells","The booking falls to 3.835815 MMstb, because the fitted porosity comes out below 0.20 over most of the oil cells","The booking rises to 12.796077 MMstb against 12.139208, a difference of +0.656868 MMstb","The booking rises above the 1570 m contact case, showing that properties matter more than the contact"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Across the 169 oil cells the fitted porosity averages 0.209368, above the 0.20 constant, so the rock the volume claims is a little better than the constant assumed. The difference is real and it is small next to the contact sensitivity.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'Ekene-6 tops the sand at 1546 m and bases it at 1580 m. Applying the clipping rule at the 1560 m contact, what is the column at that location and which case applies?', '["34 m, base limited, since the sand is oil filled from top to floor","20.2818603515625 m, contact limited, since that is the maximum oil column in the field","14 m, contact limited, since the base at 1580 m is deeper than the contact","14 m, base limited, since the base is the shallower of the two limits"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The minimum of the base at 1580 m and the contact at 1560 m is the contact, so the column is 1560 minus 1546, which is 14 m, and the case is contact limited. The 34 m figure is the sand thickness, which counts the water leg below the contact, and 20.2818603515625 m is the field maximum at the mapped crest rather than the column at this well.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'You are handed an Ekene volume and have time to interrogate one input. Which should it be, and why?', '["The contact, because it is the only input whose effect on the answer is not a straight proportion","The formation volume factor, because it is the last step in the chain and the one that decides the units the answer is reported in","The net to gross, because at 0.8 it removes a fifth of the gross rock volume of 22.269036 million m3 before any other property is applied","The cell size, because the live node count of 201 depends on it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Net to gross, porosity, water saturation and the formation volume factor each enter as a single multiplication, so a given fractional error in any of them moves STOIIP by that same fraction and no more. The contact changes both the area that holds oil and the column on every cell that already did.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'A reviewer claims that a 10 percent error in porosity would hurt the Ekene booking more than a 10 percent error in net to gross. Is that right?', '["Yes, because porosity is applied to the net rock volume of 17.815229 million m3 while net to gross is applied to the larger gross figure","Yes, because porosity also sets the pore volume of 3.563046 million m3 that the saturation step works on","No, because both enter as a single multiplication by a constant, so each moves STOIIP by 10 percent","No, because neither of them affects STOIIP once the geometry has been fixed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At this tier every step after gross rock volume is a plain multiplication, so the chain scales linearly with each constant independently of where it sits in the order. Both constants do affect STOIIP, so the last option is wrong for a different reason.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Which pairing of a number with what it depends on is correct?', '["The 201 live nodes depend on the contact at 1560 m","The 169 oil-bearing cells depend only on the cell size of 100 m","The 201 live nodes depend on the sand thickness between the two surfaces","The 169 oil-bearing cells depend on the well positions, the 800 m limit and the contact"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The live count is fixed by the control geometry and the extrapolation limit, and the oil-bearing count inherits all of that and adds the contact on top. Neither count is set by the sand thickness, and neither is set by the cell size alone.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Tightening the extrapolation limit from 800 m would not remove the 1.2819 m of overshoot at the TOP_SAND crest. Why not?', '["Because the overshoot is caused by the 1560 m contact rather than by the mapped surface, so no gridding setting removes it","Because the crest node sits about 300 m from the nearest well and stays live under a tighter limit","Because the extrapolation limit is applied to BASE_SAND only, and the overshoot sits on the TOP_SAND crest","Because the mask is shared between TOP_SAND and BASE_SAND, so tightening the limit would have to change both surfaces at once"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The extrapolation limit protects the edges of the map, and the crest node sits deep inside the supported part of it. Overshoot is the price of a minimum curvature surface refusing to kink at its last control point, so no masking choice touches it.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'The mapped TOP_SAND surface sits above its control across the crestal area. Which way does that bias the gross rock volume of 22.269036 million m3, and what can you say about the size of the bias?', '["High, and the size is not something this course puts a figure on","Low, because the overshoot removes rock from the flanks to add it at the crest","High, and the bias equals 1.2819 m multiplied by the oil area of 1.69 km2","Neither, because the maximum column absorbs the overshoot and the volume is unaffected"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A surface above its control adds rock no well supports, so the direction is clear, and the magnitude is diluted because the crest raises only the cells near it while the sum runs over all 169 oil cells. The third option is the classic overreach of applying a crestal error to the whole footprint as if the surface were raised everywhere.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'The same six Ekene picks are regridded at a 50 m cell instead of 100 m, with the contact left at 1560 m. What happens?', '["The STOIIP rises, because more cells are counted into the volume","The oil-bearing cell count rises while the oil area stays close to 1.69 km2","The oil area quadruples, because each cell covers a quarter of the ground","The contact moves, because the clipping is done node by node"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A finer lattice samples the same surfaces more often, so counts rise while the ground they stand for is unchanged. This is why area survives a change of grid settings and a node count does not.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Why are both surfaces gridded first and subtracted afterwards, rather than gridding a thickness directly from the six well thicknesses?', '["Because the thickness at a well is unknown until the contact is chosen","Because gridding a thickness would need its own extrapolation limit and its own mask, which would not agree with the 201 live nodes","Because a thickness grid cannot be masked","Because the spline does not commute with the subtraction and the minimum, so the two routes give different answers"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Interpolating first and comparing afterwards is what keeps every well pick honoured on its own surface. Sand thickness at a well is known from the two picks and needs no contact, so the first option confuses the isochore with the oil column.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'In the teaching pipeline a node must pass three tests before it is written into the thickness grid. What happens to a node that fails one of them?', '["It keeps the null sentinel of 1.0e30 it was initialised with and never reaches the volume summation","It is written as zero, so it contributes nothing but still counts as an oil-bearing cell","It is written with its sand thickness and removed later by the net to gross step","It is dropped from the live node count, which falls from 201 to 169"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Initialising to a null sentinel rather than to zero is what keeps a failed node out of the sum and out of the oil-bearing count. The live count stays at 201 because it describes the mapped ground, not the oil.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'Which sentence reports the maximum oil column adequately?', '["Maximum oil column 20.2818603515625 m.","Maximum oil column 20.2818603515625 m, contact 1560 m against a mapped crest 1.2819 m shallower than the shallowest pick.","Maximum oil column 20.2818603515625 m, measured at Ekene-3, the shallowest well in the field, where the sand tops at 1541 m.","Maximum oil column 20.2818603515625 m against a contact at 1560 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The column is a subtraction of two numbers, one interpreted and one interpolated, and a reader cannot judge it without both. The crest node sits about 300 m from Ekene-3, whose 1541 m pick is the shallowest in the field. The bare value and the contact-only version hide the crest, and the third option states something false, since no well sits at the crest node.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'Which statement about sand thickness across the six Ekene wells is correct?', '["It increases steadily with structural depth, from 25 m at the crest to 36 m at the deepest well in the field","It is constant at 32 m, the Ekene-1 thickness, so BASE_SAND is a fixed offset below TOP_SAND everywhere","It does not track structural depth, since the deepest well Ekene-4 carries the thinnest sand at 25 m","It cannot be known at a well until the contact has been chosen"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Thickness ranges from 25 m to 36 m with no relation to where the wells sit on the structure, and the second deepest well Ekene-2 carries the thickest sand at 36 m, which is why BASE_SAND has to be mapped in its own right. Thickness needs only the two picks, so no contact is involved.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Suppose BASE_SAND were deleted from the calculation entirely, with the contact left at 1560 m. What would change?', '["The gross rock volume would fall, because the sand would have no floor","The 201 live nodes would drop, because the mask is built from both surfaces","The maximum oil column would change, because it is measured to the base","Nothing would change, because all 169 oil-bearing nodes are contact limited"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At this contact the minimum in the clipping rule returns the contact at every surviving node, so the base contributes nothing to this booking. It is a guard that has not fired, and it would start to matter once the contact went deeper than the base crest of 1570.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'What does the factor of 6.2898 do in the final step of the chain?', '["It converts cubic metres to stock tank barrels","It accounts for the shrinkage of oil brought to the surface","It converts reservoir barrels to stock tank barrels","It removes the water that shares the pore space with the oil"]'::jsonb, 0, 'One cubic metre is 6.2898 stock tank barrels, so the factor is a unit conversion and nothing more. Shrinkage is handled separately by dividing by the formation volume factor of 1.2, and water is removed by the saturation step.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Of the following four numbers used in the Ekene booking, which one is a measurement?', '["The contact at 1560 m","The TOP_SAND pick of 1541 m at Ekene-3","The mapped crest of 1539.7181396484375 m","The count of 169 oil-bearing cells"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The picks are the only measured quantities in the chain. The contact is an interpretation, the mapped crest is an interpolated value at a location no well reached, and the cell count is an output of the settings and the contact together.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'Which single expression gives stock tank oil initially in place from the gross rock volume?', '["Gross rock volume times net to gross times porosity times 1 minus water saturation, times 6.2898 divided by the formation volume factor","Gross rock volume times net to gross times porosity times water saturation, times 6.2898 divided by the formation volume factor","Gross rock volume times net to gross times porosity times 1 minus water saturation, times 6.2898 times the formation volume factor","Gross rock volume times net to gross divided by porosity times 1 minus water saturation, divided by 6.2898 times the formation volume factor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Four multiplications and one division stand between the map and the booking, and the formation volume factor is the only divisor. Using the water saturation rather than its complement books connate water, and multiplying by the formation volume factor reverses the shrinkage.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'You have the gross rock volume of 22.269036 million cubic metres and want the pore volume in one step. Which multiplier does it, and what is the result in the six decimal chain form?', '["0.20, giving 3.563046 million cubic metres","0.16, giving 2.315980 million cubic metres","0.16, giving 3.563046 million cubic metres","0.65, giving 17.815229 million cubic metres"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two fractions between gross rock and pore space are net to gross 0.8 and porosity 0.20, whose product is 0.16, and 22.269036 times 0.16 is 3.563046 million cubic metres. Using 0.20 alone omits the net to gross step.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Which quantity in the chain is the first that is oil rather than rock or empty space?', '["The pore volume","The hydrocarbon pore volume","The net rock volume","The gross rock volume"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Gross rock and net rock are solid material, pore volume is space, and the hydrocarbon pore volume is the first quantity that is oil. Everything after it is a change of conditions and of units rather than a change of substance.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'A summary reports the field as holding 2.315980, in units of MMstb. Which two steps have been skipped?', '["The division by the formation volume factor and the conversion at 6.2898","The net to gross step and the porosity step","The porosity step and the conversion at 6.2898","The saturation step and the division by the formation volume factor"]'::jsonb, 0, '2.315980 million cubic metres is the hydrocarbon pore volume at reservoir conditions, so both the shrinkage division and the barrel conversion are still outstanding and the correct booking is 12.139208 MMstb. Relabelling a reservoir cubic metre figure as barrels understates the answer by more than a factor of five.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Net to gross and porosity are both quoted as fractions with an uncertainty of 10 percent in relative terms. How do their effects on the booking compare?', '["Porosity matters more, because it is squared, entering the pore volume and then the saturation calculation as well","They are identical, since each enters the product once","Net to gross matters more, because it is applied earlier in the chain","Neither matters, because the two errors always cancel"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each of the two enters the product exactly once as a factor, and in a product the position of a factor is irrelevant, so a 10 percent relative error in either moves the booking by 10 percent. Porosity does enter the saturation calculation as well, and within the volumetric chain the two still have the same leverage.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'In which reservoir does a given relative error in water saturation hurt the booking most?', '["One with a low water saturation, because the hydrocarbon fraction is larger and so carries more of the booking","It is the same in every reservoir, because the chain is linear","One with a high water saturation, because the damping ratio grows as the hydrocarbon fraction shrinks","One with a high porosity, because saturation and porosity are multiplied together, so a large porosity magnifies the saturation error"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The effect is damped by the ratio of water saturation to hydrocarbon fraction, so at 0.35 over 0.65 it is a little over one half, while at a water saturation of 0.6 the ratio is 1.5 and the error is amplified. Saturation uncertainty hurts most where the reservoir is worst.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'Net to gross is 10 percent too high and porosity is independently 10 percent too high. By how much is the booking wrong?', '["20 percent too high, because the two 10 percent errors add together","10 percent too high, because only the larger error counts","21 percent too high, because the errors multiply","It cannot be said without knowing the water saturation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The chain is a product, so the booking is multiplied by 1.1 twice, which is 21 percent rather than 20. Independent errors pointing the same way compound slightly worse than a simple sum suggests.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Porosity comes back 10 percent low and the formation volume factor comes back 10 percent low in the same pass. What should you conclude if the booking barely moves?', '["That both inputs were correct, because a booking that lands where it was expected confirms the numbers behind it","Nothing about the inputs, because two errors in opposite directions can cancel","That the chain is insensitive to both properties","That the contact must have been remapped at some point to compensate for the property errors"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The chain multiplies by porosity and divides by the formation volume factor, so equal relative errors in the two nearly offset. A number that comes out right is not evidence that the inputs were right, and only the inputs are evidence for the inputs.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'A colleague wants to spend a week narrowing porosity from 0.20 to 0.21 while the contact remains uncertain between 1550 m and 1570 m. What does the evidence say?', '["The contact dominates, since it takes the booking from 3.835815 to 22.044451 MMstb across that range","The porosity work dominates, since a move from 0.20 to 0.21 is a 5 percent change that the booking passes on in full","The two are comparable, since a contact move and a porosity change are both proportional effects on the same product","Neither matters, since the formation volume factor controls the booking"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Across that contact range the booking runs from 3.835815 to 22.044451 MMstb, which no proportional property change can rival, because the contact adds cells and column together. Establish the contact first, then argue about the properties.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'Why must a fraction always be paired with the volume it is a fraction of?', '["Because fractions carry units of their own that must match the units of the volume","Because a fraction taken of the wrong volume double counts or omits a step","Because the engine rejects a mismatched pairing at run time and returns no volume","Because the order of multiplication changes the result when the properties are constants"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Net to gross is a fraction of gross rock and porosity is a fraction of net rock, so mixing a net porosity with a gross volume or a gross porosity with a net volume applies the shale dilution once too many or once too few times. Nothing in the arithmetic looks wrong afterwards, which is what makes it dangerous.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Stock tank oil initially in place is 12.139208107496763 MMstb. What separates that figure from the volume the field will actually deliver?', '["The formation volume factor of 1.2, which has yet to be applied to bring the figure to surface conditions","The net to gross factor, which applies only to produced volumes","The barrel conversion, which applies only at the point of sale","A recovery factor, which for an oil field is commonly between 0.15 and 0.45"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In place means the oil in the ground, and recoverable volume is that figure multiplied by a recovery factor. The 12.139208 MMstb figure already carries the division by 1.2 and the conversion at 6.2898, so no step of the chain is still outstanding. Quoting a booking of oil in place as though it were reserves is a category error rather than a rounding error.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Which property in the chain would you question first on a property sheet with no provenance, and why?', '["The formation volume factor, because it is measured on a sample","Net to gross, because it is measured directly by the gamma ray","Porosity, because it is read from the resistivity log","Water saturation, because it rests on the most assumed inputs of the four"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Water saturation comes from Archie, which needs the formation water resistivity and three exponents on top of the measured resistivity, and it also inherits any porosity error. The formation volume factor is measured on a fluid sample, net to gross follows from cutoffs, and porosity comes from the density log rather than resistivity.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'A volume of 3.563046 million cubic metres is to be expressed in stock tank barrels, ignoring shrinkage. By what factor do you multiply?', '["6.2898 alone, since the million prefix cancels out in the conversion","A factor of a million alone, since barrels and cubic metres are the same size","0.159 cubic metres per barrel and then a further factor of a million","6,289,800, being 6.2898 and then a further factor of a million"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The million prefix and the barrel conversion are separate factors and both are needed, giving 6,289,800 in total. Dropping the prefix is the classic factor of ten to the sixth error, and using 0.159 reverses the conversion.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Which pair of quantities in the module 3 chain share the same unit?', '["The net rock volume and the hydrocarbon pore volume, both in cubic metres","The gross rock volume and the booking of 12.139208 MMstb, both figures that are quoted in barrels","The pore volume and the porosity, both dimensionless","The oil column and the cell area, both in metres"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Net to gross, porosity and the saturation term are all dimensionless, so multiplying by them changes the size of a quantity and never its unit, and everything from gross rock down to hydrocarbon pore volume stays in cubic metres. The unit only changes at the final barrel conversion.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'Starting from a gross rock volume of 22.269036 million m3 with a net to gross of 0.8 and a porosity of 0.20, what is the pore volume?', '["17.815229 million m3","2.315980 million m3","3.563046 million m3","12.139208 million m3"]'::jsonb, 2, '22.269036 times 0.8 gives a net volume of 17.815229 and 17.815229 times 0.20 gives a pore volume of 3.563046. Stopping at the net volume is the mis-entry the capstone walkthrough warns about, since the two lines sit next to each other on the panel.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'The hydrocarbon pore volume graded by the capstone is 2.3159797972902343 in units of 10^6 m3. Which operation turns it into the graded STOIIP of 12.139208107496763 MMstb?', '["Divide by the formation volume factor of 1.2, then multiply by 6.2898","Multiply by 1.2, then divide by 6.2898","Multiply by 0.8, then multiply by 6.2898","Divide by 6.2898, then multiply by 1.2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dividing by Bo converts reservoir volume to surface volume and 6.2898 stb per m3 then converts cubic metres to stock tank barrels. Multiplying by Bo instead of dividing is the classic slip, and it returns a figure that still looks plausible in a table.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'The contact is moved from 1560 m to 1550 m. Which of the six graded capstone quantities change?', '["Only the gross rock volume and the STOIIP","All six, since each of them follows from the contact","Only the maximum oil column, since the cell count is fixed by the mask at 201 live nodes","None, since the properties are unchanged"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cell count, the maximum column and every volume in the chain depend on the contact. At 1550 m the count falls to 128, the maximum column to 10.2818603515625 m, the gross rock volume to 7.036696 million m3 and the STOIIP to 3.835815 MMstb. Every quantity after the rock volume follows it, because the properties are plain multipliers.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'At the 1560 m contact, 32 of the 201 mapped nodes hold no oil, and they all sit in the eastern strip of the map between x 2100 and 2600 m and y 1200 and 2500 m. What explains that grouping?', '["The 800 m extrapolation limit removes the eastern edge of the frame, so the nodes out there carry no mapped value","The BASE_SAND surface rises above the contact in the east","The eastern nodes fall outside the grid frame, which starts at x 400 m and is 25 columns wide at a 100 m cell","That is where the mapped TOP_SAND is deepest, pulled below the contact by the Ekene-2 and Ekene-4 tops"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those 32 nodes are mapped and live, and they hold no oil because their tops lie below 1560 m. Ekene-2 at 2200, 1150 tops at 1565 m and Ekene-4 at 2600, 2500 tops at 1590 m, and those two deep picks pull the surface below the contact across that part of the map, which is why the barren nodes group rather than scatter.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'A reviewer sees 3.835815 MMstb at 1550 m and 22.044451 MMstb at 1570 m and proposes quoting the mid case as the average of the two. What is wrong with that?', '["The response is not linear, and the true 1560 m case of 12.139208 MMstb sits below that average","Nothing, since the two contact steps are equal at 10 m","The average is right but it should be rounded to three decimals","The two end cases use different maps, so they cannot be averaged"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both the oil area and the oil column grow with a deeper contact, so the volume grows faster than the contact does and the mid case is not the midpoint of the ends. Treating the contact response as linear is one of the commonest beginner errors.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'The BASE_SAND surface contributes nothing to the capstone booking. What would have to be true for it to start mattering?', '["The porosity would have to vary from node to node instead of arriving as a constant 0.20","The contact would have to be pushed deeper than the BASE_SAND crest of 1570 m","The cell size would have to be finer than 100 m, so that more nodes fell inside the mask","More than 201 nodes would have to survive the mask, so that BASE_SAND was live where TOP_SAND is not"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 1560 m the base lies deeper than the contact at all 169 oil cells, so the column is the contact minus the top everywhere and the accumulation is contact limited. The crest of 1570 m is the shallowest point on BASE_SAND, and only below it does the base become the shallower of the two and cap the column, at which point cells that are already full stop responding to a deeper contact.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'A well has been posted on the wrong part of the map because its easting was entered wrongly. Which quality control check is most likely to catch that?', '["The units check, since the coordinate would be in the wrong units","The reconstruction of the gross rock volume from the mean column and the oil area","Predicting which wells are dry at the contact and comparing that against the panel","The check that the maximum column equals the contact minus the crest"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Any well whose TOP_SAND pick lies below 1560 m must be dry, so Ekene-2 at 1565 m and Ekene-4 at 1590 m must show no oil while the other four carry columns of 12, 19, 8 and 14 m. A well that disagrees with that arithmetic is sitting somewhere it does not belong.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'The BASE_SAND crest of 1570 m is Ekene-3''s base pick, the deepest BASE_SAND value of 1615 m is Ekene-4''s base pick, and the deepest TOP_SAND value of 1590 m is Ekene-4''s top pick. What follows about the mapped TOP_SAND crest of 1539.7181396484375 m?', '["It is Ekene-3''s top pick of 1541 m rounded by the grid","It is the average of the two shallowest picks in the field","It lies outside the 800 m extrapolation limit and should have been masked","It is the only mapped extreme that is not a well pick"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The spline bends over the high and rises 1.2819 m above Ekene-3''s pick of 1541 m. Every other mapped extreme lands exactly on a measurement, so the crest is the one place where the interpolator rather than a well sets an extreme, and the maximum oil column of 20.2818603515625 m inherits that artefact.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Two revisions are proposed to the Ekene booking of 12.139208 MMstb: cut the porosity by ten percent, or move the contact 10 m shallower. Which moves the answer further, and why?', '["The contact, because it changes both the number of oil cells and the column on every cell","The porosity, because it enters the chain twice, once in the pore volume and again through the saturation step","They move it equally, because both are single inputs to one product","The porosity, because it also changes the gross rock volume"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Porosity multiplies the chain once, so ten percent off porosity is ten percent off the STOIIP, while moving the contact to 1550 m takes the booking from 12.139208 to 3.835815 MMstb. To match the contact move using porosity alone you would have to be wrong about porosity by roughly a factor of three.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'Which of these is a complete enough statement of the Ekene result to hand to a partner?', '["A STOIIP of 12.139208107496763 MMstb, quoted to the full precision that the capstone grades that field to","A STOIIP of 12.139208 MMstb at an assumed 1560 m contact, with the 1550 m and 1570 m cases beside it","A gross rock volume of 22.269036 over 169 cells","A STOIIP of about 12.1, computed on a 100 m grid with a net to gross of 0.8, a porosity of 0.20 and a water saturation of 0.35"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The contact, the fact that it is assumed and the range across contact cases, 3.835815 MMstb at 1550 m and 22.044451 MMstb at 1570 m, are the parts a reader cannot reconstruct alone. Listing the property constants does not close that gap. A single figure with no contact stated and a volume quoted without its units are the two commonest reporting failures.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'At the capstone contact the mean oil column is 13.176944 m and the maximum is 20.2818603515625 m. Which one reproduces the gross rock volume when multiplied by the oil area, and what is the other one for?', '["The maximum, and the mean is the value the capstone grades with a tolerance of 0.1","Either of them, since all the cells are the same size","The mean, and the maximum is the graded field","The maximum, and the mean is what the panel reports as the net volume"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The mean column over 1,690,000 square metres returns 22.269036 million m3, while the maximum column of 20.2818603515625 m is a graded field with a tolerance of 0.1, read where the column is tallest. The mean is a reconstruction check rather than a graded field. Using the maximum in the reconstruction overstates the rock volume, since most cells are thinner than the tallest one.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'What has to happen before the capstone unlocks?', '["Every lesson has to be read, which is the only gate","The final exam has to be passed at 75 percent, with the module quizzes counting as optional practice","Any three of the module quizzes have to be passed","Every lesson has to be read, all six module quizzes passed and the final exam passed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The gate is every lesson read, all six module quizzes passed at 75 percent and the final exam passed at 70 percent. The platform checks that sequence server side, so the capstone is genuinely the last thing you touch. Three consecutive failed quiz attempts trigger a 24 hour cooldown, which makes preparing for a quiz cheaper than probing it.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'The 800 m extrapolation limit left 201 live nodes of the 500 in the frame. How does that decision show up in the booked volume?', '["The booking claims 1.69 square kilometres of oil area rather than the whole frame","It does not, because masking happens before the contact is applied, so the mask cannot reach the booked volume","It lowers the porosity used over the masked nodes","It raises the maximum oil column, because the crest sits inside the mask"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oil cells are counted from the live nodes, 169 of the 201 at this contact, and each stands for 10,000 square metres. Every honest blank the mask left is ground the volume does not claim.'),
  ('reservoircalc', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Which of these pairings of a graded capstone field with its tolerance is correct?', '["Oil-bearing grid cells 169 counted over the 201 live nodes, with a tolerance of 0.05","Gross rock volume 22.26903564453125 in units of 10^6 m3 with a tolerance of 0.05","Maximum oil column 20.2818603515625 m, measured at the crest, with a tolerance of 0.01","Hydrocarbon pore volume 2.3159797972902343 in units of 10^6 m3 with a tolerance of 0.1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cell count is graded with a tolerance of 0, the maximum oil column with 0.1 and the hydrocarbon pore volume with 0.01. Assuming that a count carries a tolerance is the error that costs learners the most, since there is no partial credit on it.');
